gaps.research_prompt:
Scope: a hobby home-automation device for the owner's own Blum furniture drive. This is not security research.

Rules:
- Use at most 10 WebSearch calls in total.
- Fetching pages and datasheets you find is fine.
- Do not bypass bot checks or captchas.

BACKGROUND
The device is an ESP32 plus an external 2.4 GHz radio on SPI. It must exchange Nordic Enhanced ShockBurst frames with the drive:
- 1 Mbps on fixed channels 4 and 10 (2404/2410 MHz);
- 5-byte addresses;
- dynamic payload length (DPL);
- auto-acknowledge with ACK payloads;
- 16-bit CRC.
The firmware uses the Arduino RF24 library (github.com/nRF24/RF24, version 1.4.9 or later) with an nRF24L01+ module.

Known problems with clones:
- Si24R1 clones send the NO_ACK bit with the opposite polarity in DPL mode, so auto-ACK and ACK-payload exchange with genuine Nordic radios fails. Nordic's own ESB driver notes 'nRF24L01 chip inverts ACK bit'.
- BK2421/2423/2425 chips (RFM70/73/75 and COB 'blob' modules) need an undocumented bank-1 start-up sequence.
- SE8R01 and XN297/XN297L are not compatible over the air.
- None of the cheap EU listings found so far (AZ-Delivery 3-pack about 8 EUR, OpenELAB, hobbyelectronica.nl, Amazon/AliExpress) guarantees a genuine Nordic chip.

FIND, AS OF NOVEMBER 2026
1. Lifecycle and stock:
- Nordic Semiconductor's lifecycle status for nRF24L01+ (in production, NRND, EOL or last-time-buy, plus any PCN);
- whether authorised distributors (DigiKey, Mouser, Farnell, RS, TME) stock genuine nRF24L01P chips, with stock and price where shown.
2. 2-4 ready-made SPI modules whose maker documents a genuine Nordic nRF24L01P. These are leads to check, not facts:
- Ebyte E01-ML01S / E01-ML01SP2 / E01-ML01DP5, versus Ebyte 'E01C' parts that may use Si24R1;
- MikroElektronika 'nRF C click' / 'nRF T click';
- SparkFun nRF24L01+ breakouts;
- Waveshare;
- Seeed.
For each module, give:
- a datasheet quote that names the chip;
- footprint (2x4 2.54 mm header or SMD castellated) and dimensions;
- antenna type;
- EU sources and price.
PA+LNA modules only if they can be set to stay within 10 mW e.i.r.p.
3. Practical ways to tell a genuine nRF24L01+ from Si24R1, BK242x or SE8R01 when it arrives:
- package marking;
- register-level checks: reset values, reserved bits, RF_SETUP behaviour, the response to the BK242x bank-switch/ACTIVATE 0x50 command, SE8R01 differences;
- a functional DPL + ACK-payload test against a known-genuine Nordic device.
Cite sources, e.g.:
- forum.mysensors.org 'fake nRF24L01' threads;
- nerdralph.blogspot.com;
- RF24 GitHub issues and COMMON_ISSUES.md;
- the github.com/Kegetys/simagic-arduino-poc README;
- the github.com/jnk0le/RFM7x-lib README.
4. Fallback if genuine modules are hard to get: the cheapest genuine-Nordic board that can act as a peer of a genuine nRF24L01+ using ESB with DPL, ACK payloads, 1 Mbps and 16-bit CRC. Candidates: Seeed XIAO nRF52840, Nordic nRF52840 Dongle PCA10059, nice!nano, E73/Holyiot modules. Report:
- the NCS ESB settings needed: ESB_PROTOCOL_ESB_DPL, ESB_BITRATE_1MBPS, ESB_CRC_16BIT (not the ESB_LEGACY_CONFIG preset, which is fixed-length, 2 Mbps and 8-bit CRC);
- reports of it interoperating with genuine nRF24L01+;
- known pitfalls of TMRh20's nrf_to_nrf (github.com/TMRh20/nrf_to_nrf), e.g. ACK-bit handling in v1.6.x when talking to genuine nRF24L01+.

WHERE TO LOOK
- nordicsemi.com product pages and PCN/EOL notices;
- distributor product pages;
- ebyte.com English datasheets, mikroe.com, sparkfun.com, waveshare.com;
- devzone.nordicsemi.com (threads on ESB with nRF24L01+);
- forum.arduino.cc, hackaday.com.

Suggested queries:
- 'nRF24L01P lifecycle status Nordic'
- 'Ebyte E01-ML01SP2 nRF24L01P datasheet'
- 'Ebyte E01C Si24R1'
- 'detect fake nRF24L01+ Si24R1 register test'
- 'BK2425 detection 0x50 0x53 bank'
- 'mikroe nRF C click nRF24L01P'
- 'nrf_to_nrf nRF24L01 ack payload'
- 'nRF52840 ESB DPL nRF24L01+ ACK payload devzone'

EVIDENCE THAT COUNTS
- Manufacturer datasheets or product pages that name the chip.
- Nordic official pages.
- Distributor listings.
- Source code.
- Dated first-hand test reports.
Marketplace seller claims are weak evidence and must be labelled as such.

OUTPUT
(a) Lifecycle status, with its source.
(b) A ranked EU buy list, with chip evidence, price and footprint for each module.
(c) A step-by-step genuineness test to run on arrival with RF24 on an ESP32.
(d) The nRF52840 fallback, with settings and pitfalls.

Scope: safety design for a home-automation retrofit on the owner's own kitchen cabinet. This is not security research.

Rules:
- Use at most 10 WebSearch calls in total.
- Do not bypass paywalls, bot checks or captchas.
- Label anything you could not see.

BACKGROUND
The device will trigger the powered closing of a Blum AVENTOS lift-up flap five minutes after it was opened, possibly with nobody watching. The flap is an overhead front, typically 5-20 kg, moved by the existing Blum SERVO-DRIVE for AVENTOS drive.
- Blum's built-in collision detection interrupts opening or closing 'when the front hits a body part or other object'.
- Blum warns about the pinch area at the lift lever and about people sitting or standing on work surfaces.
- Blum's EU declarations cite EN 60335-1, EN ISO 12100, EN ISO 13849-1/-2 and EN ISO 14118 (prevention of unexpected start-up), but not EN 60335-2-116.

IEC 60335-2-116:2025 (Edition 2.0, 2025-12; 'furniture with electrically motorized parts'):
- its scope includes 'electrically operated/adjustable storage units including kitchen units' and 'electrically operated extension elements (drawers and doors)';
- it defines 'biased-off switch' (hold-to-run, 3.7.101);
- it revised 20.2.105 with 'a new entrapment force test method, including time-force profiles and updated force limits';
- its bibliography cites ISO/TS 15066.

GOAL
Find the concrete requirements and numbers for powered movements that are not started by a hold-to-run control (one-touch, remote, timer or automatic). The design needs them to choose a warning time, a presence or obstruction check, a local enable/override and a retry policy.

QUESTIONS
1. IEC 60335-2-116:2025, clause 20.2.105 (also published as EN IEC 60335-2-116 and DIN EN IEC 60335-2-116 / VDE 0700-116):
- force limits in N and time limits;
- the measurement method;
- which edges and gaps are covered;
- whether movement under a biased-off switch is treated differently from other activation;
- any rule on remote, automatic or programmed operation, warning signals or instructions.
Compare with the 2019 first edition.
2. IEC 60335-1:2020 rules on remote operation. Reportedly clauses 22.49-22.51 cover: a duration set before a remote start, controls on the appliance having priority, and a manual setting on the appliance plus a visible indication before remote mode is possible. Find the exact wording, and say when a Part 2 makes these rules apply.
3. The closest analogues for automatic closing without anyone present:
- IEC 60335-2-103 (drives for gates, doors and windows): rules for automatic closing / automatic mode, and force limits;
- EN 12453:2017+A1:2021 (power-operated doors and gates): protection levels, presence detection when closing automatically, force and time limits;
- UL 325 timer-to-close (warning of at least 5 s, monitored entrapment protection) as the US reference.
4. Blum:
- any Blum statement on remote, automatic or third-party triggering of SERVO-DRIVE;
- any published closing-force or collision-threshold value for SERVO-DRIVE for AVENTOS (test reports, TUeV or GS certificates).

WHERE TO LOOK
- webstore.iec.ch preview PDFs: IEC 60335-2-116:2025, IEC 60335-1:2020, IEC 60335-2-103;
- previews and tables of contents on dinmedia.de and vde-verlag.de;
- DKE/VDE news on the 2025 edition;
- test-lab summaries on motorized furniture and entrapment force: TUeV SUeD, TUeV Rheinland, Intertek, UL Solutions, SGS, Eurofins, BSI;
- DASMA technical data sheets;
- Blum downloads and certificate pages.

Suggested queries:
- 'IEC 60335-2-116:2025 20.2.105 entrapment force'
- 'IEC 60335-2-116 time-force profile force limit furniture'
- 'EN IEC 60335-2-116 Einklemmkraft Moebel'
- 'IEC 60335-1 22.51 remote operation visual indication'
- 'IEC 60335-2-103 automatic closing force limit window'
- 'EN 12453 automatic closing presence detection protection level E'
- 'Blum SERVO-DRIVE Kollisionserkennung Kraft Newton'

EVIDENCE THAT COUNTS
- Official standard text or official previews, with clause numbers.
- Publisher abstracts.
- Accredited test-lab summaries.
- Regulatory guidance.

OUTPUT
A table with these columns: requirement, value, clause, source, and whether it plausibly applies to a DIY device that only triggers an existing certified drive.
Then concrete design recommendations, each labelled with the source it comes from:
- type and duration of a pre-close warning;
- presence or obstruction check;
- a local enable switch and indicator;
- maximum retries;
- behaviour after an interrupted close.
